      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. I noticed you mentioned Scalia's view as being tied to the 'collective right' and the militia, but Heller actually shifted that toward an individual right based on originalist principles. \n\nTo help my students understand the depth of this, could you go deeper into the specific legal logic used by originalists to justify the individual right interpretation? I'd like to see how they use historical texts to build their case, and then contrast that with the core logical framework living constitutionalists use to argue that the amendment must be interpreted through a modern lens.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalists justifying the individual right interpretation of the Second Amendment appeal to historical texts to suggest an evolving understanding of the right. They often refer to the English Bill of Rights (1689), which influenced the American founders, indicating a broader concept of self-defense within the right to bear arms. Originalists also analyze Federalist Papers, particularly Federalist No. 46, where James Madison emphasizes that an association for the common defense (essentially, a militia) should not preclude individual rights to keep and bear arms. Justice Scalia, leading the majority in Heller, argued that the term \"the people\" inherently includes individuals. He saw phrases like \"a well-regulated militia\" as a modifier of the right, not an independent substitute for it, and posited the right to self-defense as a fundamental freedom untouched by historical context.\n\n\nContrasting this is the modern framework that living constitutionalists like Breyer propose. They emphasize contemporary understandings of the Second Amendment's function in ensuring an armed citizenry capable of preventing tyranny. Breyer's perspective as articulated in Heller dissents and subsequent opinions focuses on the backdrop of changing social and governmental contexts, advocating for a broader application of the right to self-defense in line with the amendment's practical",
      "source": "target",
